      {
        "index": 3115,
        "name": "Dry Conversation",
        "description": "MENU: \u201cI swear I saw them rattle.\u201d\nMINA: \u201cHe must be excited to speak.\u201d\nWET BONES: \u201cThe Herman Miller Action Office plan was the world\u2019s first open-plan modular workspace, designed in 1964 by Robert Propst and George Nelson.\u201d\nWET BONES: \u201cThis system was conceptualized after extensive research into how knowledge workers operate.\u201d\nWET BONES: \u201cThe original design intention was to liberate workers\u2019 minds and maximize their potential.\u201d\nMENU: \u201cWhat is he going on about? Worker potential?\u201d\nWET BONES: \u201cHeight-adjustable desks to stand or sit. Movable partitions for temporary privacy.\u201d\nWET BONES: \u201cIn a twisted inversion, budget cuts and design revisions over time led to the cubicle farms of the 90s.\u201d\nWET BONES: \u201cA dry and lonely prison of fabric walls.\u201d\nWET BONES: \u201cThe only other person I really saw was the girl at the front desk.\u201d\nWET BONES: \u201cI\u2019d stop and take a hard candy. Make some conversation.\u201d\nWET BONES: \u201cHard candy...\u201d\nWET BONES: \u201cMake some conversation\u2026\u201d\nMINA: \u201cHe somehow makes more and less sense at the same time.\u201d\nMENU: \u201cTry giving him some hard candy, I guess.\u201d\nMINA: \u201cA lot of it.\u201d",
